"1\n00:00:00,500 --> 00:00:03,711\nThe Learning Brain. Series 6: Neuromyths\n\n2\n00:00:06,100 --> 00:00:07,314\nEpisode 9\n\n3\n00:00:07,394 --> 00:00:10,743\nMultiple intelligences in the classroom\n\n4\n00:00:13,543 --> 00:00:14,278\nLinguistic.\n\n5\n00:00:14,413 --> 00:00:15,929\nLogical mathematical.\n\n6\n00:00:15,977 --> 00:00:16,602\nSpatial.\n\n7\n00:00:16,622 --> 00:00:17,417\nMusical.\n\n8\n00:00:17,483 --> 00:00:18,929\nBodily kinesthetic.\n\n9\n00:00:19,205 --> 00:00:20,171\nInterpersonal.\n\n10\n00:00:20,191 --> 00:00:21,157\nIntrapersonal.\n\n11\n00:00:21,177 --> 00:00:22,110\nNaturalist.\n\n12\n00:00:22,361 --> 00:00:23,556\nEight kinds of smart.\n\n13\n00:00:23,986 --> 00:00:26,065\nMany teachers plan lessons around them.\n\n14\n00:00:26,085 --> 00:00:26,884\nShould they?\n\n15\n00:00:27,054 --> 00:00:34,665\nIn 1983, psychologist Howard Gardner proposed multiple intelligences: separate abilities,\n\n16\n00:00:34,899 --> 00:00:37,015\neach with its own brain basis.\n\n17\n00:00:37,420 --> 00:00:41,686\nIt was a generous idea that challenged a single number for intelligence,\n\n18\n00:00:41,897 --> 00:00:43,408\nand teachers embraced it.\n\n19\n00:00:43,578 --> 00:00:46,008\nBut the theory makes testable claims.\n\n20\n00:00:46,322 --> 00:00:48,259\nIf the intelligences are separate,\n\n21\n00:00:48,408 --> 00:00:51,292\nscores on them should be largely independent.\n\n22\n00:00:51,808 --> 00:00:55,046\nStudies found the opposite: they tend to go together.\n\n23\n00:00:55,396 --> 00:00:59,509\nAnd researchers have not found distinct brain systems for each one.\n\n24\n00:00:59,679 --> 00:01:03,513\nIn reviews in 2006 and 2023,\n\n25\n00:01:03,849 --> 00:01:08,050\nLynn Waterhouse concluded that the theory lacks empirical support,\n\n26\n00:01:08,502 --> 00:01:14,260\nand that studies of teaching to the intelligences were not done by standard scientific methods.\n\n27\n00:01:14,749 --> 00:01:20,864\nEven so, one survey found ninety percent of American teacher trainees planned to use it.\n\n28\n00:01:21,034 --> 00:01:23,275\nGardner himself has drawn a line.\n\n29\n00:01:23,781 --> 00:01:27,431\nHe says multiple intelligences are not learning styles,\n\n30\n00:01:27,686 --> 00:01:32,384\nand that matching lessons to a student's intelligence is a misreading of his work.\n\n31\n00:01:32,554 --> 00:01:36,884\nThe grain of truth: people do have different strengths and interests.\n\n32\n00:01:37,220 --> 00:01:39,643\nAnd showing an idea in more than one way,\n\n33\n00:01:39,823 --> 00:01:41,395\na graph, an equation,\n\n34\n00:01:41,415 --> 00:01:44,589\na story, can deepen understanding for everyone.\n\n35\n00:01:44,759 --> 00:01:45,927\nDo this instead.\n\n36\n00:01:46,375 --> 00:01:49,373\nChoose representations because the content needs them,\n\n37\n00:01:49,587 --> 00:01:51,270\nnot to match student types.\n\n38\n00:01:51,614 --> 00:01:55,458\nValue many kinds of talent without sorting students into boxes.\n\n39\n00:01:55,727 --> 00:01:59,130\nAnd put planning time into methods with strong evidence.\n\n40\n00:01:59,566 --> 00:02:05,123\nOne tip to share with students: use many representations because the content needs them,\n\n41\n00:02:05,342 --> 00:02:07,022\nnot because you are a type.\n\n42\n00:02:07,192 --> 00:02:09,045\nMany ways to show an idea,\n\n43\n00:02:09,065 --> 00:02:09,505\nyes.\n\n44\n00:02:09,773 --> 00:02:11,015\nEight kinds of student,\n\n45\n00:02:11,035 --> 00:02:11,362\nno.\n\n"